#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134653} :activity/id #uuid "e9ed40f2-8404-5db3-8f11-b43aa4b94833" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048828 :topic/id #uuid "c3f4792c-c754-5f17-936f-e303d32dc1ec" :topic/math-academy-id 2153} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134653 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186135040 :tutorial/content "Remember that two vectors $\\mathbf{u}$ and $\\mathbf{v}$  in $\\mathbb{R}^n$ are **orthogonal** if their dot product equals zero: $\\mathbf{u}\\cdot \\mathbf{v}=0.$\n\nThe **Pythagorean Theorem** provides another way to determine whether two vectors are orthogonal. It states the following:\n\n> Two vectors $\\mathbf{u}$ and $\\mathbf{v}$ in $\\mathbb{R}^n$ are orthogonal if and only if $\\,\\Vert \\mathbf{u}+\\mathbf{v}\\Vert^2 = \\Vert \\mathbf{u} \\Vert^2 + \\Vert \\mathbf{v}\\Vert^2.$\n\nFor instance, consider the vectors $[\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]$ and $[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]$ These vectors are orthogonal since\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nIndeed, because $\\mathbf{u}$ and $\\mathbf{v}$ are orthogonal, the Pythagorean theorem holds true:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖u+v{‖}^{2} & =‖u{‖}^{2}+‖v{‖}^{2} \\\\ {(8+1)}^{2}+{(-2+4)}^{2} & =((8)^{2}+(-2)^{2})+((1)^{2}+(4)^{2}) \\\\ {9}^{2}+{2}^{2} & =(64+4)+(1+16) \\\\ 81+4 & =68+17 \\\\ 85 & =85\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe Pythagorean theorem for vectors is really just the same concept as the Pythagorean theorem from geometry. To understand how, notice that we can visualize $\\mathbf{u}+\\mathbf{v}$ as the hypotenuse of a right triangle whose legs are $\\mathbf{u}$ and $\\mathbf{v}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n![](images/25/25beac642edd3e89ec005f4a6a555f6bd31632e74a3d295320bfd11315b68c47.png)\n\n**Important:** The Pythagorean theorem is true only if the vectors are *orthogonal*!" :tutorial/id #uuid "a3cd72b6-ccb3-5016-8f37-4c19d4ae2db1" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3613 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "7b057b17-9456-53a3-84ab-c873f1a33a00" :step/math-academy-id 20214 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134654}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134654 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133775 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133584 :question/id #uuid "0c2486d6-bb55-52ba-9fd8-6b516d3f7858" :question/math-academy-id "e-5557"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4aa4b13a-c222-5a32-b289-04b8c8285d89" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048849} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049224}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134216} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134217} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326823} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326824} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342152} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342153}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Norm of a Vector Using the Pythagorean Theorem"} :step/id #uuid "df1d2ca8-62a5-51ea-a0be-314557040975" :step/math-academy-id 20215 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134655}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134655 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186135042 :tutorial/content "Another inequality relating the norms of two vectors is the **triangle inequality**, which states the following:\n\n> Let $\\mathbf{u}$ and $\\mathbf{v}$ be vectors in $\\mathbb{R}^n.$ Then $\\Vert \\mathbf{u} + \\mathbf{v} \\Vert \\leq \\Vert \\mathbf{u} \\Vert + \\Vert \\mathbf{v} \\Vert.$\n\nFor instance, consider the vectors $[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$ and $[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]$ Indeed, the triangle inequality holds true for these vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖u+v‖ & \\le ‖u‖+‖v‖ \\\\ \\sqrt{{(6+2)}^{2}+{(0+4)}^{2}} & \\le \\sqrt{{6}^{2}+{0}^{2}}+\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+{4}^{2}} \\\\ \\sqrt{64+16} & \\le \\sqrt{36}+\\sqrt{4+16} \\\\ \\sqrt{80} & \\le 6+\\sqrt{20} \\\\ 4\\sqrt{5} & \\le 6+2\\sqrt{5} \\\\ 8.9… & \\le 10.4…\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe triangle inequality for vectors is really just the same concept as the triangle inequality from geometry. To understand how, notice that we can visualize $\\mathbf{u}+\\mathbf{v}$ as the third side in a triangle whose other two sides are $\\mathbf{u}$ and $\\mathbf{v}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n![](images/11/11af705440c67bf62678b4ad2399af649dbaa65037b3c32c69d3e803dc2f1c7b.png)\n\nGeometrically, the triangle inequality states that it is always quicker (or at least not longer) to travel directly along the vector $\\mathbf{u}+\\mathbf{v}$ than it is to travel first along the vector $\\mathbf{u}$ and then along the vector $\\mathbf{v}.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "e5bf3368-ddc3-57ea-b1b4-09ab8aded935" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3615 :tutorial/title "The Triangle Inequality"} :step/id #uuid "32bef78d-d382-524c-9e4e-7ccea73af02f" :step/math-academy-id 20216 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134656}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134656 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133776 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133585 :question/id #uuid "112253ae-e0ea-554a-83bf-87f8ba7c9ce7" :question/math-academy-id "e-5558"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0ae3aec8-4d20-5467-9ced-2a9c1bda3afd" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050235}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134264} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134275} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272096} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272097} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326766} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326889} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326890} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326891} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342171}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Minimum Value Using the Triangle Inequality"} :step/id #uuid "9a1aa9a5-75d2-5ca0-a7bd-994776409822" :step/math-academy-id 20217 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134657}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134657 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133777 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133586 :question/id #uuid "5e72235b-a419-5f27-bee7-01be8b49c997" :question/math-academy-id "e-5559"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2391612a-ee27-5cb2-a194-9b51e6fec1e1" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050693}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134219} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134225} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272098} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272099} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326768} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326821} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326822} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342174} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342175} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342177}] :knowledge-point/title "Combining the Pythagorean Theorem and the Cauchy-Schwarz and Triangle Inequalities"} :step/id #uuid "f53ad40e-5583-5922-a65b-1f63ddc5acf9" :step/math-academy-id 20218}] :activity/title "The Pythagorean Theorem and the Triangle Inequality" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186134022}]}
